                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "mid-side":</h3><p><b>Mid Side</b> is a stereo miking technique in which a cardioid element is paired with a figure-of-8 element to produce a stereo recording. The cardioid element takes the &#8220;mid&#8221; channel, and is pointed (usually) at the sound source; the bidirectional element is pointed so that the sound source is at the capsule&#8217;s null point &#8212; in other words, it points at 90&deg; to the direction of the cardioid capsule. Ideally, the two capsules should be at a single point in space, or as close as their headbaskets will allow.</p><p>The &#8220;side&#8221; channel (meaning, the output of the figure-8 mic) is then mult&#8217;ed to two tracks in the DAW or mixer. Pan these hard R and L, and flip the phase on one of them. </p><p>Adjusting the volume of the &#8220;mid&#8221; channel in relation to the &#8220;side&#8221; channels affects the perceived width of the stereo image; the louder the &#8220;mid,&#8221; the narrower the stereo effect. </p><p>The other advantage of Mid-Side or &#8220;M-S&#8221; stereo is that the signal degrades to mono without phase cancellation. This means that a M-S stereo track will be less likely to change its relative volume when played back in mono, as compared to an XY stereo track.</p><p>Read more about <a href="http://www.wikirecording.org/Mid-Side_Microphone_Technique">mid-side stereo recording</a>.</p>
